## Solution

**Substitution.** Let
$$a = r,\quad b = \frac{s}{r},\quad c = \frac{t}{s},\quad d = \frac{4}{t}.$$
Then $a,b,c,d \ge 1$ (since $1\le r\le s\le t\le 4$) and
$$abcd = r\cdot\frac{s}{r}\cdot\frac{t}{s}\cdot\frac{4}{t}=4.$$
Conversely, any $a,b,c,d\ge 1$ with $abcd=4$ gives $r=a,\; s=ab,\; t=abc\le 4$ (since $d=4/t\ge 1$), so the substitution is a bijection. The objective becomes
$$F=(a-1)^2+(b-1)^2+(c-1)^2+(d-1)^2,\qquad a,b,c,d\ge 1,\; abcd=4.$$

**Lagrange multiplier (interior critical points).** At an interior minimum,
$$\frac{\partial}{\partial a_i}\!\left[\sum(a_j-1)^2-\lambda\!\left(\prod a_j-4\right)\right]=0 \implies 2(a_i-1)=\lambda\prod_{j\ne i}a_j=\frac{4\lambda}{a_i},$$
so $a_i(a_i-1)=2\lambda$ for every $i$. Hence every $a_i$ is a root of $x(x-1)=2\lambda$, i.e. $a_i\in\{x_+,x_-\}$ with $x_\pm=\tfrac{1\pm\sqrt{1+8\lambda}}{2}$. For $\lambda>0$, $x_-<0<1$, violating $a_i\ge 1$; thus all $a_i$ are equal: $a=b=c=d=4^{1/4}=\sqrt2$.

**Value at the symmetric point:**
$$F=4(\sqrt2-1)^2=4(3-2\sqrt2)=12-8\sqrt2\approx 0.6863.$$

**Boundary analysis.** If $k$ of the variables equal $1$, the remaining $4-k$ variables (each $\ge 1$, product $4$) minimize $\sum(x_i-1)^2$. By the same Lagrange argument the interior optimum is all equal at $4^{1/(4-k)}$:

| $k$ | value |
|-----|-------|
| $0$ | $4(\sqrt2-1)^2=12-8\sqrt2\approx 0.686$ |
| $1$ | $3(4^{1/3}-1)^2\approx 3(0.587)^2\approx 1.034$ |
| $2$ | $2(4^{1/2}-1)^2=2$ |
| $3$ | $(4-1)^2=9$ |

Every boundary value exceeds $12-8\sqrt2$.

**Existence of a minimum.** The feasible set $\{a_i\ge 1,\prod a_i=4\}$ is closed, and $F\to\infty$ as any $a_i\to\infty$, so $F$ attains its minimum. Since the unique interior critical point yields a value strictly smaller than every boundary value, it is the global minimum.

**Conclusion.** The minimum value is

$$\boxed{12-8\sqrt2}.$$

